<GuideLayout 
  title="5 Red Flags to Watch When Sourcing Activewear from China"
  type="SOURCING STRATEGY"
  description="Avoid common pitfalls. Learn how to identify real factories versus trading companies and protect your brand's supply chain."
>
  <p>Finding a reliable partner in China requires due diligence. Watch out for these warning signs.</p>
  <h3>1. Vague Technical Knowledge</h3>
  <p>If they can't explain the GSM of a fabric or the difference between flatlock and twin-needle stitching, they are likely a middleman.</p>
  <h3>2. Unrealistic Pricing</h3>
  <p>Prices that are 40% below market average usually mean sub-standard fabrics or poor ethical conditions.</p>
  <h3>3. Lack of Certifications</h3>
  <p>Always ask for valid ISO, BSCI, or SGS reports. Legitimate factories will provide these without hesitation.</p>
  <h3>4. Poor Communication</h3>
  <p>B2B manufacturing requires clear, fast communication. Long delays in response can lead to production disasters.</p>
  <h3>5. No Physical Presence</h3>
  <p>If a supplier refuses a video tour of the production line or doesn't have a verified factory address, move on. Transparency is non-negotiable.</p>
</GuideLayout>
